{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-62069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/b9668f2d9988488c64e2bb7edb55ae94b1b84b947e249fbc96c506b1c5095d4d.png)\n\nA manufacturer produces a triangular prism-shaped box to hold chocolate candies. In the figure, the areas of some of the faces are displayed in a net of the prism. What is the total surface area of the box?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-62069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The surface area of our prism consists of $2$ triangles (base and top) and $3$ rectangles (central and side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =2\\times (\\text{Area of the base})+2\\times (\\text{Area of the side faces}) \\\\  & +1\\times (\\text{Area of the central face}) \\\\  & =2\\times 24+2\\times 45+54 \\\\  & =48+90+54 \\\\  & =192\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-62069" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4476aeb61c12d8e89fd68a7bbc8e33f29f7cb8ca4f1cc18fc60380fae96ce2b"} {"question" "q-62069" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-62069" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "124\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}"] ["math" "148\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}"] ["math" "154\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}"] ["math" "172\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}"] ["math" "192\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-62069" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "192\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-40030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The domain of the function $f(x)=\\frac{8}{2x+b}$ is $(-\\infty ,4)\\cup (4,+\\infty ).$ What is the value of $b?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-40030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A reciprocal function is defined for all values of $x$ except for those that make the denominator zero. So, to find the domain, we must exclude all values that make the denominator zero.\n\nSetting the denominator equal to zero and solving, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+b & =0 \\\\ 2x & =-b \\\\ x & =-\\frac{b}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the point that must be excluded from the domain is $x=-\\frac{b}{2}.$ \n\nOn the other hand, we can see that $x=4$ is excluded from the domain. Equating this to $-\\frac{b}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{b}{2} & =4 \\\\ -b & =8 \\\\ b & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-40030" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4476aeb61c12d8e89fd68a7bbc8e33f29f7cb8ca4f1cc18fc60380fae96ce2b"} {"question" "q-40030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-40030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-6"] ["math" "-8"] ["math" "10"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-40030" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-325983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/8e6ccf522b5325c3b592ca9470810a95c5491f0b68f000ea774f6555cee0f47f.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. If $c=\\lambda a-2b,$ then\n\n$\\lambda =$\n{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-325983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/646b8f1322f815e786f82eb8cfb3fa9e84a3c8d588c94910b0b702b607bfb2d8.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{3}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-\\frac{3}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-2b-\\frac{3}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-2b=-\\frac{3}{2}a-2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{3}{2}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-325983" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4476aeb61c12d8e89fd68a7bbc8e33f29f7cb8ca4f1cc18fc60380fae96ce2b"} {"question" "q-325983" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-325983" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-1916" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $f(x)={x}^{2}{e}^{x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-1916" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Here, we have the product of two functions $u(x)={x}^{2}$ and $v(x)={e}^{x}.$ So, we apply the product rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =(2x)({e}^{x})+({x}^{2})({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =2x{e}^{x}+{x}^{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =(x+2)x{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-1916" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4476aeb61c12d8e89fd68a7bbc8e33f29f7cb8ca4f1cc18fc60380fae96ce2b"} {"question" "q-1916" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-1916" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+1)x{e}^{x}"] ["math" "(x+2)x{e}^{x}"] ["math" "2{x}^{2}{e}^{x}"] ["math" "x(2{e}^{x}+x)"] ["math" "x(x{e}^{x}+2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-1916" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x+2)x{e}^{x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-102889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "How many different codes can be formed by the characters ${y,z,2,4,6}$ besides \"$yz246$\"? Each character can be used only once." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-102889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The total number of the characters is $5$, so there are $5!$ ways of arranging them:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5! & =5\\times 4\\times 3\\times 2\\times 1 \\\\  & =120.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe code \"$yz246$\" is one of them. By subtracting $1$ from $120,$ we find the number of the different codes that can be formed beside \"$yz246$\".\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120-1=119\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-102889" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4476aeb61c12d8e89fd68a7bbc8e33f29f7cb8ca4f1cc18fc60380fae96ce2b"} {"question" "q-102889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-102889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "119"] ["math" "120"] ["math" "124"] ["math" "128"] ["math" "132"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-102889" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "119"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-150014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the tangent to the curve $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+1)$ at the point $(0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-150014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we express $f(x)$ as the composite function\n\n$$\nf=\\operatorname{ln}⁡u,\\,u=3x+1.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=3.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3x+1}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope $m$ of the tangent line at the point where $x=0$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={\\frac{3}{3x+1}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{3(0)+1} \\\\  & =3\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the slope of the tangent line at the point $(0,0)$ is $m=3.$ Now, we find the equation of the tangent at that point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =3\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =3x\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-150014" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4476aeb61c12d8e89fd68a7bbc8e33f29f7cb8ca4f1cc18fc60380fae96ce2b"} {"question" "q-150014" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-150014" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=3x"] ["math" "y=3x+1"] ["math" "y=3x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3"] ["math" "y=x"] ["math" "y=x+3"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-150014" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=3x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-317288" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following is the graph of $y=-{5}^{x}+4?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-317288" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To plot the given function, we follow these steps:\n\n- We take the curve $y={5}^{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/09798cb8c62aabe483490ad24f4c13918138c4292ffb19a8d6612ab4efe18497.png)\n\n- Reflect it on the $x$-axis to give $y=-{5}^{x}.$ The $y$-intercept moves from $(0,1)$ to $(0,-1)$ but the horizontal asymptote stays at $y=0.$\n\n- Then, vertically shift it by $4$ units up to give $y=-{5}^{x}+4.$ The point $(0,-1)$ moves to $(0,3)$ and the horizontal asymptote moves from $y=0$ to $y=4.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-317288" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b4476aeb61c12d8e89fd68a7bbc8e33f29f7cb8ca4f1cc18fc60380fae96ce2b"} {"question" "q-317288" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-317288" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/4e70aa30c11b1576699fa384e830d9ad1608963abb0ee98e0cd961d6afb2762c.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/72c4b7567924115d3717a14a98d5a1806687b68a6dafe7d102927d98e0d2a4c3.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/78e1b3933cf1048d32fba849cf1fac5e8ef049fc0714cdf0512d912c9875424f.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/b7c6f2453026a6cdc4dbd91806108513a4522f3aa42bc73e4dcc68e1ae0013f9.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/f70c7f91f1164b564fea4582928392748a150e63f66b8038b7aad007e2760034.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"} {"question" "q-317288" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/assets/f70c7f91f1164b564fea4582928392748a150e63f66b8038b7aad007e2760034.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13981901/state.json" "file_sha256" "7b30ab80983d21449110a6709ec6a412a2477d5191b6e61455e02b1e214fdaef"}] "usage" {} "basis" 899 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
